# response.gbnf — grammar-constrains hexward-ai's output to exactly the
# JSON shape internal/aiclient.Explanation expects (see spec §4):
#
#   {"explanation": "...", "what_to_verify": ["...", "..."], "disclaimer": "..."}
#
# This is loaded by llama.cpp's server via --grammar-file, or passed
# per-request in the "grammar" field of /v1/chat/completions (the field
# internal/aiclient.WithGrammar attaches). It is enforced ENTIRELY inside
# llama.cpp's own token sampler — hexward-ai does not post-process or
# retry model output to "fix" invalid JSON, because a grammar that
# actually constrains sampling never produces invalid JSON to fix.
#
# What this grammar guarantees: syntactically valid JSON in the required
# shape, with the three required keys present, in this order, with
# "what_to_verify" always an array of strings (possibly empty).
#
# What this grammar does NOT guarantee, and cannot: that the content of
# "explanation" only restates facts from the evidence packet. That is a
# prompting/instruction-following concern (see the system prompt in
# internal/aiclient/client.go and spec §7), not a grammar concern — a
# grammar constrains shape, never truthfulness. Two independent layers
# exist because of this: the grammar makes the JSON parseable, the prompt
# tries to make the content grounded, and internal/aiclient's client
# additionally overwrites the "disclaimer" field itself rather than
# trusting the model to have copied it correctly.

root ::= ws "{" ws
  "\"explanation\"" ws ":" ws string ws ","  ws
  "\"what_to_verify\"" ws ":" ws string-array ws "," ws
  "\"disclaimer\"" ws ":" ws string ws
  "}" ws

string-array ::= "[" ws "]" | "[" ws string ("," ws string)* ws "]"

string ::= "\"" char* "\""
char ::= [^"\\\x00-\x1F] | "\\" (["\\/bfnrt] | "u" hex hex hex hex)
hex ::= [0-9a-fA-F]

ws ::= [ \t\n\r]*
